# Set these with the config editor, `scripts/config.sh` (site: The config
# editor): it shows each variable's documentation below and checks every change
# with the binaries' own loaders. To edit by hand instead, copy this file to
# .env and fill it in.

# Postgres, as every binary on this machine reaches it. The containers always
# use db:5432 over the compose network. The password in it is a credential.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://judgebot:judgebot@localhost:5432/judgebot
# Where compose publishes Postgres on the host (loopback only). Change both
# this and the port in DATABASE_URL if 5432 is taken.
#DB_PORT=5432
# What the judgebot process runs, as launch flags (at least one):
#   --discord  the Discord bot (needs DISCORD_TOKEN and JUDGE_OPERATOR_DISCORD)
#   --api      POST /api/judge, the question route the web app calls
#   --web      the web app (the image carries it)
#   --mcp      the MCP transport at /mcp (needs MCP_TOKEN)
#   --jobs     the scheduled data refresh (JUDGE_REFRESH_HOURS)
# --api, --web and --mcp need JUDGE_OPERATOR_EMAIL. Unset, the compose service
# runs `--discord --api --web --jobs`. Without Discord (trying it out, or a
# public page only): '--api --web --jobs'. `cargo run -p judgebot` reads this
# when its command line names no role. Keep the outer quotes: every binary
# reads .env with dotenvy, which refuses an unquoted value with spaces. Put no
# quotes inside the value: Compose splits it as a shell would, judge-config
# and the binary on whitespace alone.
#JUDGE_ROLES='--discord --api --web --jobs'
# The zero-config model setup (no judge.toml): Anthropic's first-party API for
# both stages, Voyage for embeddings if keyed (blank = the vector search
# is off, and the bot still works). ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL, VOYAGE_MODEL and
# VOYAGE_DIMENSIONS (default voyage-3.5 / 1024) are the only other knobs this
# setup reads. Everything else needs a judge.toml.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
VOYAGE_API_KEY=
#ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com
#VOYAGE_MODEL=voyage-3.5
#VOYAGE_DIMENSIONS=1024
# The Discord bot's token (Developer Portal > your application > Bot). Only the
# bot reads it. It is a credential: whoever has it can act as the bot.
DISCORD_TOKEN=
# Discord (crates/bot). Members holding a role named JUDGE_ROLE rate as judges
# (their score overrides crowd votes).
JUDGE_ROLE=Judge
# A server id: the bot registers its six commands in that server only, where
# they appear at once. Other servers the bot is in, and DMs with it, get no
# commands (unless an earlier global registration was left in place). Blank
# registers them globally: every server the bot is in, and DMs for all but
# /judge, within up to an hour. Switching leaves the other set registered.
GUILD_ID=
# judgebot applies pending schema migrations at startup (the migrations are
# built into the image). Set false to manage the schema yourself with
# `docker compose run --rm refresh migrate` or sqlx-cli.
JUDGE_AUTO_MIGRATE=true
# Most /judge runs in flight at once. Further ones get a "busy" reply.
JUDGE_CONCURRENCY=2
# Discord only: /judge questions one member may ask per window, so one person
# cannot spend the cap (default 6 per 600 s; JUDGE_USER_LIMIT=0 turns it off).
# /card and /rule lookups call no model and are not limited.
JUDGE_USER_LIMIT=6
JUDGE_USER_WINDOW_SECS=600
# Model spend cap in USD (default 5), across every provider, embeddings
# included. The cap reserves the worst case before each call, so values under
# ~0.36 refuse synthesis outright. Once the headroom left is smaller than a
# call's worst case, questions are refused. What the cap covers is
# JUDGE_BUDGET_PERIOD. `judgebot ingest` (a reembed) is capped by it too; raise
# it for one run with `docker compose run --rm -e JUDGE_MAX_USD=20 refresh ...`.
JUDGE_MAX_USD=5
# What JUDGE_MAX_USD caps:
#   process (default)  each judgebot process, for its lifetime, all its roles
#                      together. A restart counts from zero.
#   day | month        the current UTC day or month, shared by every process
#                      through the database and kept across restarts. Questions
#                      resume by themselves when the next period starts.
# judgebot ingest follows the period too. judge-cli and judge-eval always cap
# per process.
JUDGE_BUDGET_PERIOD=
# Hours between data refreshes (Scryfall cards and rulings, a new
# Comprehensive Rules release, embeddings, emoji), run by a process with the
# --jobs role (the compose service runs it): default 24, 1 to 720,
# 0 = off. Every process and a cron'd
# scripts/refresh-data.sh take turns through a lock in the database, so a
# refresh runs once however many there are. A failed refresh is retried
# after an hour, then less often. A scheduled run never embeds more than 800 rows: above that it
# skips the embedding and alerts, and `judgebot ingest embed` does it by hand.
# A bad value stops every binary that reads this file. In a development .env,
# set 0: `cargo run -p judgebot` with --jobs would otherwise refresh your local
# database for real (downloads, a new CR, embeddings, emoji uploads).
JUDGE_REFRESH_HOURS=24
# Optional: an https webhook (Discord, Slack or compatible) told the first time
# the cap refuses a question in a period, when a scheduled refresh fails (once
# per streak), recovers or leaves rows unembedded, and when
# scripts/refresh-data.sh or scripts/backup-db.sh fails. The `backup` service
# reads its own copy from .env.deploy. The URL is a credential: whoever has it
# can post to the channel.
JUDGE_ALERT_WEBHOOK=
# Optional: a judge.toml choosing providers and models per stage. The choices
# are Anthropic (direct, via a proxy, or on AWS/Bedrock/Vertex with the
# platform's own credentials), any OpenAI-compatible server, and a local
# Ollama. See judge.example.toml. This is a host path, relative to the repo
# root or absolute. `cargo run` reads it directly and docker-compose.yml
# bind-mounts it into judgebot and refresh. Blank: `cargo run` reads ./judge.toml
# if present, else the setup above. The containers see only what compose
# mounts, never the repo root, so under Docker a ./judge.toml is invisible
# until this names it.
JUDGE_CONFIG=
# Provider secrets are named by the file, never written in it. Each
# `api_key_env = "NAME"` in judge.toml reads NAME from here. For every provider
# a stage names it must be set and non-blank in every process that loads the
# file. That includes `ingest refresh`, which resolves the chat stages even
# though it only embeds. A table no stage names is parsed but its key is never
# read. Add one line per provider you configure, e.g.
# LITELLM_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# The Anthropic cloud endpoints take no key. `endpoint = "claude-platform-on-aws"`
# and "bedrock" sign with AWS's default chain: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID +
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY (+ AWS_SESSION_TOKEN) here, or AWS_PROFILE with a
# mounted ~/.aws (AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE / AWS_CONFIG_FILE name the
# files), or an instance/container role. "vertex" uses Application Default
# Credentials: GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS naming a mounted service-account
# JSON, or the metadata server on GCE. The chain is probed once at startup, so
# a missing credential fails there, naming the provider and the endpoint.

# Where the network roles (--api, --web, --mcp in JUDGE_ROLES) listen, all on
# one port, with GET /api/health and GET /api/about served whichever of them
# is on. API_ADDR must stay 0.0.0.0 in Docker so cloudflared can reach it
# across the compose network. The published port is what restricts access,
# not this.
API_ADDR=0.0.0.0:8787
# Read only under --web, and a --web launch whose WEB_DIST holds no index.html
# is refused at startup rather than serving 404s. The image sets its own.
WEB_DIST=web/dist
# The API answers anonymously: per-IP rate limiting (API_RATE_LIMIT questions
# per API_RATE_WINDOW_SECS) protects API spend.
# API_CLIENT_IP picks what the rate limiter buckets on: "peer" (default, the
# socket address) or "cloudflare" (CF-Connecting-IP, which Cloudflare always
# overwrites and callers cannot forge). Set it to cloudflare only when a
# Cloudflare tunnel is the sole route to the API. X-Forwarded-For is never
# trusted. Cloudflare *appends* to a caller-supplied header rather than
# replacing it, so its first hop is attacker-chosen.
API_RATE_LIMIT=4
API_RATE_WINDOW_SECS=300
API_CLIENT_IP=peer
# MCP over HTTP (crates/agent mounted by judgebot at /mcp). The endpoint needs
# both the `--mcp` role and this token. `--mcp` without a token is refused
# at startup, because it would be an anonymous route to the judge pipeline. A
# token without `--mcp` serves nothing and only warns, so a leftover token
# never takes the web app down. Unset (and no --mcp) = no MCP
# endpoint. Set a long random token (`openssl rand -base64 32`, at least 24
# characters) and give it to your MCP client as `Authorization: Bearer ...`.
# The tools include `judge` (spends model budget under JUDGE_MAX_USD and
# JUDGE_CONCURRENCY, shared with the web app) and agent-driven sessions (no
# model calls). MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS lists the Host header values the MCP
# transport accepts, comma-separated. The list replaces the default, so name
# the public hostname behind the tunnel AND localhost if you curl on the host,
# e.g. mtgjudge.example.com,localhost. Empty keeps the loopback-only default.
# MCP_JUDGE_LIMIT `judge` runs per MCP_JUDGE_WINDOW_SECS through /mcp: the
# exposure of a leaked token. The web app's per-IP limit does not apply.
MCP_TOKEN=
MCP_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
MCP_JUDGE_LIMIT=20
MCP_JUDGE_WINDOW_SECS=3600

# The source offer every remote interface makes (the web app's footer and
# GET /api/about, Discord /help and /license, the MCP instructions and `about`
# tool): the repository holding this instance's source, shown with the commit
# the binary was built from and the AGPL-3.0-or-later notice. Blank = the
# upstream repository, right for an unmodified build. If you run a modified
# version, point this at your fork. The AGPL requires offering your users the
# source of what they are talking to, and with this set every interface does.
# Must be an http(s) URL. Anything else is refused at startup.
JUDGE_SOURCE_URL=

# Who runs this instance. Each surface refuses to start without its own.
# JUDGE_OPERATOR_DISCORD: your Discord username (not the display name, no
#   name#1234 tag; a leading @ is fine). Required by --discord, which shows it
#   in /help and /license.
# JUDGE_OPERATOR_EMAIL: a support address. Required by --api, --web and --mcp,
#   whichever of them run: shown by GET /api/about, the web app's footer and the
#   /mcp instructions. Letters, digits and ._+- before the @.
# A surface shows the other contact too when it is set. judge-cli and judge-mcp
# on stdio need neither. A value that is set and malformed is refused everywhere.
JUDGE_OPERATOR_DISCORD=
JUDGE_OPERATOR_EMAIL=

# --- Deployment: Cloudflare Tunnel (docs/DEPLOYMENT.md) -------------------
# Set COMPOSE_PROFILES=tunnel on the server so `docker compose up -d` also
# starts cloudflared. Leave it empty for local development.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=
# Which published image judgebot runs. JUDGE_IMAGE is the package (blank = the
# upstream ghcr.io/sloshy/mtg-judgebot; a fork sets its own ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>
# once its Publish image workflow has run). JUDGE_IMAGE_TAG blank means :latest,
# which follows every push to main. A release version (1.0, 1.0.0) follows or pins
# releases. A `sha-<short>` tag from that workflow pins one build or rolls back.
# After changing it: `docker compose pull && docker compose up -d --remove-orphans`.
JUDGE_IMAGE=
JUDGE_IMAGE_TAG=
